## 1. Identify the Attached EBS Volume

The AWS CLI was used to locate the running EC2 instance and identify its root volume.

```bash
aws ec2 describe-instances \
  --region us-east-1 \
  --filters "Name=tag:Name,Values=devops-ec2" \
  --query 'Reservations[].Instances[].{InstanceId:InstanceId,State:State.Name,RootDevice:RootDeviceName,Volumes:BlockDeviceMappings[].{Device:DeviceName,VolumeId:Ebs.VolumeId}}' \
  --output table
```

### Preview — Identified EC2 Instance

```text
InstanceId: i-03aab5edeca62bd07
State: running
RootDevice: /dev/xvda

Device: /dev/xvda
VolumeId: vol-015bea10961b22b3f
```

The root EBS volume was successfully identified.

## 2. Expand the EBS Volume

The attached EBS volume was modified from 8 GiB to 12 GiB.

```bash
aws ec2 modify-volume \
  --region us-east-1 \
  --volume-id vol-015bea10961b22b3f \
  --size 12
```

### Preview — Volume Modification

```text
VolumeId: vol-015bea10961b22b3f
ModificationState: modifying
OriginalSize: 8
TargetSize: 12
```

The modification status was checked using:

```bash
aws ec2 describe-volumes-modifications \
  --region us-east-1 \
  --volume-ids vol-015bea10961b22b3f \
  --query 'VolumesModifications[].{VolumeId:VolumeId,OriginalSize:OriginalSize,TargetSize:TargetSize,State:ModificationState}' \
  --output table
```

Observed status:

```text
OriginalSize: 8
TargetSize: 12
State: optimizing
```

AWS confirmed that the volume modification had progressed successfully.

## 3. Connect to the EC2 Instance Using SSH

The public IP address was retrieved using:

```bash
aws ec2 describe-instances \
  --region us-east-1 \
  --instance-ids i-03aab5edeca62bd07 \
  --query 'Reservations[0].Instances[0].PublicIpAddress' \
  --output text
```

SSH key permissions were restricted:

```bash
chmod 400 /root/devops-keypair.pem
```

The connection was established using:

```bash
ssh -i /root/devops-keypair.pem ec2-user@52.91.15.110
```

The SSH connection was successful, providing access to the Amazon Linux 2023 instance.

## 4. Inspect the Disk and Root Filesystem

The initial disk layout was inspected using:

```bash
lsblk
df -h /
```

### Preview — Before Partition Expansion

```text
NAME      SIZE TYPE MOUNTPOINTS
xvda       12G disk
├─xvda1     8G part /
├─xvda127   1M part
└─xvda128  10M part /boot/efi
```

Initial filesystem usage:

```text
Filesystem      Size  Used Avail Use% Mounted on
/dev/xvda1      8.0G  1.6G  6.5G  19% /
```

The EBS disk had expanded to 12 GiB, but the root partition and filesystem still reflected the original 8 GiB capacity.

The filesystem type and availability of the partition expansion utility were checked:

```bash
df -T /
which growpart
```

Observed output:

```text
Filesystem     Type
/dev/xvda1     xfs

/usr/bin/growpart
```

This confirmed that the root filesystem used XFS and that `growpart` was installed.

## 5. Expand the Root Partition

The root partition was expanded to use the available disk capacity.

```bash
sudo growpart /dev/xvda 1
```

### Preview — Successful Partition Expansion

```text
CHANGED: partition=1
start=24576
old: size=16752607 end=16777183
new: size=25141215 end=25165791
```

The partition expansion completed successfully.

## 6. Expand the XFS Filesystem

The XFS filesystem was grown online using:

```bash
sudo xfs_growfs /
```

### Preview — Successful Filesystem Expansion

```text
data blocks changed from 2094075 to 3142651
```

The filesystem successfully expanded to use the additional partition capacity.

The operation did not require unmounting the root filesystem or rebooting the instance.

## 7. Final Verification

The final disk layout and filesystem capacity were verified using:

```bash
lsblk
df -h /
```

### Preview — Final Disk Layout

```text
NAME      SIZE TYPE MOUNTPOINTS
xvda       12G disk
├─xvda1    12G part /
├─xvda127   1M part
└─xvda128  10M part /boot/efi
```

### Preview — Final Root Filesystem

```text
Filesystem      Size  Used Avail Use% Mounted on
/dev/xvda1       12G  1.6G   11G  13% /
```

The final output confirms that the root partition and mounted root filesystem both recognize the expanded 12 GiB capacity.

